/*
# SimpleLayout — stack children along an axis

The stacking idiom: siblings placed one after another along
`axis`, in child order, `spacing` apart (negative overlaps). The cross axis
and all sizes stay the children's own — a child centers itself across a row
with the ordinary `y = center`. Invisible children are skipped and their
space reclaimed; a skipped child still gets the place it would occupy, so
re-showing it needs no special case. A child with `flexes = true` takes a
share of the run's slack along the axis: a list that fills the room between a
header and a footer, or an empty `Spacer` (between two groups it pushes them
apart; one on each side centers the run).

**How a run shares its room.** Every child keeps its own size along the axis,
except the ones that flex or shrink. If the run is too long for the view, the
children with `shrinks = true` give way, in proportion to their size, down to
their `minWidth` (`minHeight` in a column). If room is left, the children that
flex share it by weight (`flexes = 2` takes twice what `true` takes), each
within its range. A run that only shrinks needs a view with a size of its own;
sized from these children, it is never short.

Written in Declare over the Layout kernel: the whole strategy is this one
pure place() — one box per laid child, aligned by index.

**Padding is the view's** (`View.padding`), and this stack obeys it without
knowing it exists: it arranges inside the room the view gives it, so a run
placed at `0` starts at the content origin, the cross-axis band `align` works
in is the room *between* the insets, and a view that sizes itself from the
stack grows by both. The inset reaches **every** child, on both axes,
`align = none` included — a child placing its own `x = 0` sits on the content
edge, not the view's, because the content box is the parent's and not this
arrangement's.

```declare
col: View [ x = 20, y = 20,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 8 ],
    View [ width = 160, height = 24, cornerRadius = 6, fill = steelblue ],
    View [ width = 120, height = 24, cornerRadius = 6, fill = cadetblue ],
    View [ width = 190, height = 24, cornerRadius = 6, fill = slategray ]
    ]
```

## axis
Which way children stack: `y` (the default) or `x`. **A bare literal, not a string** —
`axis = x`, and note that `x` here is the axis token, not the position attribute.

## spacing
The gap between adjacent children. **Negative overlaps them**, which is how a stack of
cards or avatars is built.

## align
The **cross axis** — `y` on a row, `x` on a stack. `none` (the default) leaves it to the
children, who place themselves with the ordinary `y = center`. `start | center | end`
aligns every child within the **band**: the view's own cross **content** extent — so a
column `width = 260` centres a `width = { parent.width - 32 }` child at 16, with the slack
split evenly — falling back to the **line**, the tallest (widest) laid child's extent, on a view
that derives its own cross size from these very children (reading an extent this
arrangement feeds would be a cycle, and on such a view the line *is* that extent). A
child wider than the band keeps the line, so it never hangs out both sides.
`stretch` makes every child its content sizes as large as the line across the
flow — the row as tall as its tallest child, a menu's items as wide as its widest —
within each child's range; a child that states its own size keeps it (an avatar of
32 beside bubbles that stretch). The line is counted from each child's content, so
it shrinks again when the tallest one does.
`baseline` — a row only — sits every child on one
baseline: a `Text` reports its own, and a composite **declares** which part carries it
(`baseline: number = { cap.y + cap.baseline }`); a laid child that declares none is
refused by name, at the child, and the checker says so before anything runs. A
flexing child is aligned like any other (a `Spacer` declares a baseline of 0, so a
baseline row takes it as it is). A row of figures and words that must wrap when the view
is narrow — "4 sessions · 3h 40m" on a phone — is a `WrappingLayout [ align = baseline ]`,
which keeps each line on its own baseline. A bare literal.

## place()
The whole strategy: one box per laid child, positioned along the axis in child order.
**Every size stays the children's own, except along the axis for a child that flexes
or shrinks, and the cross axis stays theirs unless `align` says otherwise** — then the
boxes carry that attribute too, and own it; under `stretch` a box carries a `stretch`
size, which fills only a child its content sizes. Override it in a subclass
for custom geometry; a `Layout`'s `place()` **may** aggregate over children, which is the
one place that is allowed.
*/

class SimpleLayout extends Layout [
    axis: Axis = y,
    spacing: number = 0,
    align: CrossAlign = none,

    place() {
        const axis = this.axis
        const size = axis == "x" ? "width" : "height"
        const kids = this.laid()
        // a child the run sizes along the flow — it flexes (a weight above 0)
        // or shrinks — has its flow-axis size written here, so its box is never
        // read as a footprint (that reads both sizes): across the flow it is its
        // own raw extent, untransformed, and it aligns like any child
        const weight = (c) => {
            const f = (c as any).flexes
            return f === true ? 1 : (typeof f == "number" && f > 0 ? f : 0)
            }
        const shrinking = (c) => weight(c) == 0 && (c as any).shrinks === true
        const flexing = (c) => weight(c) > 0 || shrinking(c)
        const minKey = axis == "x" ? "minWidth" : "minHeight"
        const maxKey = axis == "x" ? "maxWidth" : "maxHeight"
        const clamp = (c, v) => {
            const hi = (c as any)[maxKey]
            const lo = (c as any)[minKey]
            const capped = hi > 0 ? Math.min(v, hi) : v
            return Math.max(capped, lo)
            }

        // the sizes the run gives, in the model's order: every other child keeps
        // its own; if they do not fit, the shrinking children give way in
        // proportion to their size, down to their minimums; if room is left, the
        // flexing ones share it by weight, within their ranges. A child's slot is
        // its footprint (the scale/rotation-composed box), so a scale = 0.5 child
        // occupies half the run and a rotated card reserves what it visibly
        // covers; the box's lead compensates the transform's own offset so the
        // visible edge lands on the slot
        const given = kids.map((c) => 0)
        let flexN = 0
        let shrinkN = 0
        let visN = 0
        let fixed = 0
        kids.forEach((c, i) => {
            if (!c.visible) return
            visN = visN + 1
            if (weight(c) > 0) { flexN = flexN + 1; given[i] = clamp(c, 0) }
            else if (shrinking(c)) { shrinkN = shrinkN + 1; given[i] = clamp(c, (c as any)[axis == "x" ? "contentWidth" : "contentHeight"]) }
            else fixed = fixed + (c as any).footprint()[size]
            })
        if (flexN + shrinkN > 0) {
            fixed = fixed + this.spacing * Math.max(0, visN - 1)
            // the room: a run that flexes divides the view's own; one that only
            // shrinks needs a view with a size of its own (on a view sized from
            // these children there is no shortage, and reading it is a cycle)
            const own = flexN > 0 ? this.contentExtent(size) : this.viewExtent(size)
            const room = flexN == 0 && own <= 0 ? Infinity : own
            let used = fixed
            kids.forEach((c, i) => { used = used + given[i] })
            const held = kids.map((c) => false)
            if (used > room) {
                // proportional to each size, down to the minimum: one that would
                // go below it holds there, and the rest share what is still over
                let over = used - room
                for (let round = 0; round <= kids.length; round = round + 1) {
                    let basis = 0
                    kids.forEach((c, i) => { if (c.visible && shrinking(c) && !held[i]) basis = basis + given[i] })
                    if (basis <= 0) break
                    let again = false
                    kids.forEach((c, i) => {
                        if (!c.visible || !shrinking(c) || held[i]) return
                        const lo = (c as any)[minKey]
                        if (given[i] - over * given[i] / basis < lo) {
                            over = over - (given[i] - lo)
                            given[i] = lo
                            held[i] = true
                            again = true
                            }
                        })
                    if (!again) {
                        kids.forEach((c, i) => { if (c.visible && shrinking(c) && !held[i]) given[i] = given[i] - over * given[i] / basis })
                        break
                        }
                    }
            } else if (flexN > 0) {
                // by weight, within each range: a share outside its range holds at
                // the bound, and the rest share again what is left
                let free = room - fixed
                kids.forEach((c, i) => { if (c.visible && shrinking(c)) free = free - given[i] })
                for (let round = 0; round <= kids.length; round = round + 1) {
                    let total = 0
                    kids.forEach((c, i) => { if (c.visible && weight(c) > 0 && !held[i]) total = total + weight(c) })
                    if (total <= 0) break
                    const each = Math.max(0, free) / total
                    let off = 0
                    kids.forEach((c, i) => {
                        if (!c.visible || weight(c) == 0 || held[i]) return
                        const want = each * weight(c)
                        given[i] = clamp(c, want)
                        off = off + given[i] - want
                        })
                    if (Math.abs(off) < 0.001) break
                    kids.forEach((c, i) => {
                        if (!c.visible || weight(c) == 0 || held[i]) return
                        const want = each * weight(c)
                        if ((off > 0 && given[i] > want) || (off < 0 && given[i] < want)) {
                            held[i] = true
                            free = free - given[i]
                            }
                        })
                    }
                }
            }

        // the cross axis, when `align` claims it: children align within the
        // BAND — the view's own cross extent, or the largest laid cross extent
        // when the view measures these very children (viewExtent answers 0 on
        // that view: reading an extent this pass feeds is the cycle the
        // one-pass discipline forbids, and there the two agree anyway).
        // Math.max keeps an over-wide child from hanging out both sides.
        // A baseline line is the deepest declared one.
        const al = this.align
        const cross = axis == "x" ? "y" : "x"
        const crossSize = axis == "x" ? "height" : "width"
        const crossOf = (c) => {
            if (flexing(c)) return ({ lead: 0, extent: (c as any)[crossSize] })
            const fc = (c as any).footprint()
            return ({ lead: axis == "x" ? fc.y : fc.x, extent: fc[crossSize] })
            }
        let line = 0
        let lineBase = 0
        // stretch: a child its content sizes counts by that content, never by the
        // size a stretch gave it (that would hold the line up once it shrank)
        const natural = axis == "x" ? "contentHeight" : "contentWidth"
        const extentOf = (c) => al == "stretch" && this.sizedByContent(c, crossSize) ? (c as any)[natural] : crossOf(c).extent
        if (al != "none") {
            if (al == "baseline" && axis != "x") this.refuseStackBaseline()
            for (const c of kids) {
                if (!c.visible) continue
                line = Math.max(line, extentOf(c))
                const b = (c as any).baseline
                if (al == "baseline" && typeof b == "number") lineBase = Math.max(lineBase, b)
            }
            // `start` places at the band's origin and `baseline` on the shared
            // line — neither reads the band, so only center/end ask for it
            // a view with a cross size of its own: center, end and stretch work in it
            if (al == "center" || al == "end" || al == "stretch") line = Math.max(line, this.viewExtent(crossSize))
        }

        let pos = 0
        return kids.map((c, i) => {
            const flex = c.visible && flexing(c)
            // footprint, never bounds: the strategy must not read the attribute it
            // writes (one-pass discipline). A flexing child's size is allocated
            // (box.w/h), so its footprint is never consulted — lead 0, raw attribute.
            const f = flex ? null : (c as any).footprint()
            const lead = f == null ? 0 : (axis == "x" ? f.x : f.y)
            const box = (axis == "x" ? ({ x: pos - lead }) : ({ y: pos - lead })) as any
            if (flex) box[axis == "x" ? "w" : "h"] = given[i]
            if (al != "none") {
                // start = the line's origin; center/end offset the child's
                // extent within the line; baseline sits the child's declared
                // baseline on the line's — a child with none is refused, once,
                // and placed at start (the arrangement never breaks)
                const co = crossOf(c)
                const leadC = co.lead
                let off = 0
                if (al == "center") off = (line - co.extent) / 2
                else if (al == "end") off = line - co.extent
                else if (al == "baseline" && axis == "x") {
                    const b = (c as any).baseline
                    if (typeof b == "number") off = lineBase - b
                    else if (c.visible) this.refuseBaseline(c)
                }
                box[cross] = off - (al == "baseline" ? 0 : leadC)
                // every child its content sizes is made as large as the line;
                // one that states its size keeps it (the runtime skips it)
                if (al == "stretch") box.stretch = axis == "x" ? ({ h: line }) : ({ w: line })
            }
            if (c.visible) pos = pos + (flex ? given[i] : (f as any)[size]) + this.spacing
            return box
            })
        }
    ]
